---
import ChronicleLayout from '../../layouts/ChronicleLayout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/chronicle/ArticleHeader.astro';
import Chapter from '../../components/chronicle/Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/chronicle/Figure.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/chronicle/SectionHeading.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/chronicle/FurtherReading.astro';
import ArticleFAQ from '../../components/chronicle/ArticleFAQ.astro';
import AtAGlance from '../../components/chronicle/AtAGlance.astro';
import Practical from '../../components/chronicle/Practical.astro';
import PracticalSection from '../../components/chronicle/PracticalSection.astro';
import type { ChronicleMeta } from '../../components/chronicle/types';
import { SIZES_MODERN_WIDE } from '../../lib/responsive-image-config.mjs';

const meta: ChronicleMeta = {
  title: 'Best Things to Do in Santiago',
  headline: 'Best Things to Do in Santiago',
  description:
    'A first-hand guide to the best things to do in Santiago, Chile, from the historic centre and Vega Central to museums, parks and the city’s viewpoints.',
  path: '/best-things-to-do-in-santiago/',
  ogImage: '/images/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1200w.jpg',
  photography: true,
};

const IMG = '/images/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary';
const landscape = (name: string) =>
  `${IMG}/${name}-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/${name}-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/${name}-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/${name}-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/${name}.jpg 2048w`;
---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Chile', 'Santiago', 'South America']}
    label="Guide · Santiago"
    title="Best Things to Do in Santiago"
    standfirst="Santiago changes character as the traveller moves through it: civic buildings become working markets, museums open into parks, neighbourhoods carry the city’s memory, and the hills turn the whole route into geography."
    stats={[
      { num: '5', label: 'Perspectives' },
      { num: '4', label: 'Nights' },
      { num: '3', label: 'Viewpoints' },
      { num: '1', label: 'City' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Santiago becomes clearer through its contrasts. The formal centre gives way
          to the market, the museums open into the parks, older neighbourhood streets
          lead away from civic buildings, and the hills return the whole city in one
          view. A first visit works best when those parts are allowed to stand beside
          one another.
        </p>
        <p>
          Begin near La Moneda and the Metropolitan Cathedral, where public buildings,
          central streets and the Mapocho edge establish Santiago’s civic face. Then
          cross towards Vega Central for the working city, and move through the
          museums, Parque Forestal and Yungay before choosing a view from San
          Cristóbal, Santa Lucía or Sky Costanera.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Santiago skyline with the mountains beyond the city."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Santiago’s centre, hills and mountain edge in one view."
      priority
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="how-to-use" rule={false}>Choose your Santiago</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        With one day, stay central. La Moneda, the Metropolitan Cathedral, the main
        streets and Vega Central make a compact introduction, with one museum if time
        allows. With two days, add the museum-and-park route and one viewpoint. With
        four days, follow the complete itinerary through Yungay, the Sound Museum,
        the remaining viewpoints and the onward bus to Mendoza.
      </p>
      <p>
        Access, opening hours, fares and transport arrangements change. Check those
        details before setting out, particularly when building several stops into one
        day.
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <AtAGlance
    id="santiago-glance"
    title="Santiago at a Glance"
    meta={[
      { label: 'Best first stop', value: 'The civic centre around La Moneda, the Metropolitan Cathedral and the central streets.' },
      { label: 'For the working city', value: 'Cross towards Vega Central, where Santiago shifts from monuments to markets and everyday movement.' },
      { label: 'For culture', value: 'Pair one or two museums with Parque Forestal rather than trying to collect every institution in a single day.' },
      { label: 'For the view', value: 'Choose San Cristóbal for hills and mountains, Santa Lucía for the central city, or Sky Costanera for the broadest skyline.' },
    ]}
    daysHeading="Five Ways into the City"
    days={[
      { num: 'Civic Centre', detail: 'La Moneda · Cathedral · Estación Mapocho', text: 'Santiago at its most formal: government, religion, public space and the historic centre. The best place to begin a first visit.' },
      { num: 'Vega Central', detail: 'Markets · Produce · Working Santiago', text: 'The city at street level. Less monumental, more immediate, and the clearest contrast with the formal centre nearby.' },
      { num: 'Museums', detail: 'Pre-Columbian Art · Fine Arts · Sound Museum', text: 'Three very different collections. Choose one or two rather than turning the day into a museum checklist.' },
      { num: 'Parks & Neighbourhoods', detail: 'Parque Forestal · Yungay', text: 'The quieter urban layer between institutions and residential streets. Best explored as part of a wider walking route.' },
      { num: 'Viewpoints', detail: 'San Cristóbal · Santa Lucía · Sky Costanera', text: 'Three different readings of Santiago: mountain geography, central-city scale and the modern skyline.' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter id="historic-centre" number="I." name="Begin with the civic centre" epithet="La Moneda, the Cathedral, Estación Mapocho and Santiago’s public face" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The historic centre gives the visit its first outline. La Moneda, the
        Metropolitan Cathedral, the central streets and Estación Mapocho sit within a
        route that moves between government, religion, public space and the city’s
        older transport edge. The pleasure is not in collecting façades. It is in
        watching Santiago change from one institution to the next.
      </p>
      <p>
        La Moneda Cultural Center belongs to this opening sequence, while the
        Cathedral anchors the central streets with a different kind of public memory.
        Estación Mapocho widens the route towards the river and the market districts.
        Together they make a better first introduction than a single landmark viewed
        in isolation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-central-park-monument.jpg`}
      srcset={landscape('santiago-central-park-monument')}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A monument in a tree-lined Santiago city setting."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="A central Santiago civic scene, where formal buildings and public space meet."
    />

    <Chapter id="vega" number="II." name="Follow the working city" epithet="Vega Central and the movement of stalls, produce and people" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Vega Central changes the texture of the day. After the civic centre’s stone
        and formal lines, the market brings the visit down to stalls, produce and
        movement. It is one of the clearest moments when Santiago stops presenting
        itself as a set of monumental places and becomes a city being used.
      </p>
      <p>
        The market works best alongside the central city, then the museums and parks.
        No particular purchase or dish needs to carry the visit. Vega matters because
        it shows Santiago being used, close to the institutions and public spaces that
        give the centre its more formal character.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-market-street.jpg`}
      srcset={landscape('santiago-market-street')}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A colourful Santiago market street with a small shop and painted walls."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="A market-side street in Santiago, away from the formal centre."
    />

    <Chapter id="museums" number="III." name="Enter the museum city" epithet="Pre-Columbian Art, Fine Arts and the Sound Museum" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Santiago’s museums give the journey an interior life. The Chilean Museum of
        Pre-Columbian Art reaches back before the Spanish period; the National Museum
        of Fine Arts returns the route to the city’s cultural centre; the Sound Museum
        carries it into a more specialised collection. They are different visits, but
        together they prevent the city from becoming only streets, markets and views.
      </p>
      <p>
        Choose the collection that most interests you, leave room for Parque Forestal
        and check current access before going. The museums work best as part of the
        surrounding streets and park, with enough time to see them as part of the city
        rather than as isolated stops.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Chapter id="park-route" number="IV." name="Walk into parks and neighbourhoods" epithet="Parque Forestal, Yungay and the less monumental side of the city" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Parque Forestal changes the pace of the museum route. It gives the day a place
        to continue on foot, with the cultural institutions no longer treated as
        isolated appointments. The park sits between the city’s formal buildings and
        the neighbourhoods that carry the day into a different urban texture.
      </p>
      <p>
        Yungay extends that movement beyond the formal centre. The Sound Museum and
        Cementerio General can extend the day here, where Santiago becomes less
        monumental. Walk this part of the city as an extension of a day that already
        has shape, rather than turning it into a broad claim about its barrios.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-cultural-building.jpg`}
      srcset={landscape('santiago-cultural-building')}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="An older Santiago building facade with balconies and flags."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="An older Santiago facade on the cultural and neighbourhood side of the route."
    />

    <Chapter id="viewpoints" number="V." name="See Santiago from above" epithet="San Cristóbal, Santa Lucía and Sky Costanera" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The viewpoints are not interchangeable. San Cristóbal brings the hills and the
        mountain edge into the frame. Santa Lucía keeps the view close to the central
        city. Sky Costanera turns the perspective towards the built skyline. Each one
        answers a different question about where Santiago sits and how it spreads.
      </p>
      <p>
        One is enough for a short visit; two make the contrast clearer. The separate
        <a href="/santiago-viewpoints/">Santiago Viewpoints guide</a> compares them
        directly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-hill-sunset.jpg`}
      srcset={landscape('santiago-hill-sunset')}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Santiago’s wooded hills and skyline beneath a pink evening sky."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Santiago’s wooded hills against the mountain edge."
    />

    <Practical place="Santiago — Things to Do" detail="First visit · Five perspectives" detailIcon="🧭">
      <PracticalSection label="Shortest route to a great first visit" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>One day</strong> — Historic centre, Vega Central and one viewpoint. Begin with La Moneda and the Cathedral, cross into the market district, then finish above the city.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Two days</strong> — Add the museum-and-park route. Choose the Pre-Columbian Art Museum or Fine Arts, walk Parque Forestal, then spend more time around the central neighbourhoods.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Three days</strong> — Add Yungay and the Sound Museum, with enough room to slow the route down rather than stacking more central attractions.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Four days</strong> — Follow the <a href="/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/">full Santiago itinerary</a>, using the extra time for the remaining viewpoints and neighbourhood stops.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>If you only choose three things</strong> — The civic centre, Vega Central and one viewpoint. Together they show Santiago at street level, institutional scale and from above.</div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="What each part of Santiago does best" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>History and civic Santiago:</strong> La Moneda, Metropolitan Cathedral and the historic centre.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Markets and everyday city:</strong> Vega Central and the streets around the Mapocho edge.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Museums and culture:</strong> Pre-Columbian Art Museum, Fine Arts and the Sound Museum.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Views and geography:</strong> San Cristóbal, Santa Lucía and Sky Costanera.</div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Watch-outs" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Do not turn the museums into a checklist.</strong> One or two strong collections will make a better day than racing through every institution on the page.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>The viewpoints are alternatives, not duplicates.</strong> San Cristóbal gives the strongest sense of Santiago against the mountains, Santa Lucía keeps the city close, and Sky Costanera gives the broadest urban panorama.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Santiago works in clusters.</strong> Walk within the centre, market district and park corridors, then use transport when the route jumps between wider parts of the city.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Keep current access in mind.</strong> Museum hours, funicular or cable-car operations and viewpoint access can change, so check live information before building a tight schedule.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <div class="narrative">
      <SectionHeading id="priorities">What deserves priority</SectionHeading>
      <div class="narrative prose">
        <p>
          <strong>With one day:</strong> stay near the centre. Combine La Moneda, the
          Metropolitan Cathedral and the central streets with Vega Central, then choose
          one museum if time allows. The day will contain civic Santiago, the working
          market and a first cultural layer without trying to cross the whole city.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>With two days:</strong> add Parque Forestal and the museum route, then
          choose one viewpoint. San Cristóbal suits the hill-and-mountain perspective;
          Santa Lucía keeps the view central; Sky Costanera gives the broadest built-up
          skyline.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>With four days:</strong> use the <a href="/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/">complete Santiago itinerary</a>
          for the full sequence through Yungay, the Sound Museum, the remaining stops
          and the departure to Mendoza.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      <SectionHeading id="surprised">Where Santiago Comes Together</SectionHeading>
      <div class="narrative prose">
        <p>
          Santiago holds these contrasts together: public institutions beside working
          streets, museum interiors opening towards parks, older neighbourhoods leading
          away from the centre, and hills that make the city’s geography visible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FurtherReading
      label="Continue through Santiago"
      cards={[
        { href: '/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/', region: 'Chile · Santiago', title: '4 Days in Santiago: Complete Itinerary', excerpt: 'The full four-night sequence through the civic centre, Vega Central, museums, parks, Yungay, the viewpoints and the bus to Mendoza.', meta: ['Itinerary', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/santiago-viewpoints/', region: 'Chile · Santiago', title: 'Santiago Viewpoints: San Cristóbal, Santa Lucía and Sky Costanera', excerpt: 'A direct comparison of San Cristóbal Hill, Santa Lucía Hill and Sky Costanera.', meta: ['Viewpoints', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/24-days-in-south-america-itinerary/', region: 'South America · Chile', title: '24 Days in South America', excerpt: 'The wider journey from La Paz through Santiago and Mendoza before the road turns east.', meta: ['Pillar itinerary', '2026'] },
      ]}
    />
  </div>
  <ArticleFAQ page="best-things-to-do-in-santiago" />
</ChronicleLayout>
